# Backend and API Engineering

- Inspect the actual backend framework, routing, error handling, auth middleware, data-access patterns and API conventions first.
- Validate request parameters, body, query and external provider responses at boundaries. Use the project's established schema-validation library when available.
- Enforce authentication, authorization, ownership and per-user isolation in server-side services/queries. Test cross-user access denial.
- Use consistent response/error contracts, timeouts, cancellation where useful, bounded retries with backoff, idempotency for retried writes and rate limits.
- Keep provider-specific code behind adapters. Normalize provider responses and retain provenance, source identifiers and timestamps.
- Protect background jobs against duplicate delivery, partial failure and retry storms. Make jobs observable and safe to replay.
- Use parameterized database access, transactions for multi-step invariants and explicit validation for state transitions.
- Do not log resumes, tokens, passwords, raw inbox contents or sensitive payloads. Redact provider errors and user identifiers appropriately.
- Document API contracts and error cases. Test authentication, authorization, validation, idempotency and failure paths, not only happy paths.